Article 5 (Measures by Chief Prosecutor of District Prosecutors' Office)
(1) Upon completion of the investigation of an objection case, the chief prosecutor of a district prosecutors' office shall take one of the following measures:
1. No grounds: Where there is no fault in the handling of affairs by the notary public concerned;
2. Request for caution: Where the notary public concerned improperly performed duties;
3. Warning: Where there is any matter, whether in or outside the performance of duties, that is inappropriate for the status of a notary public. In such case, the notary public shall be given an opportunity to explain before a warning is issued;
4. Report of occurrence of grounds for disciplinary action: Where the notary public concerned is deemed to have grounds for disciplinary action falling under any subparagraph of Article 82 (1) of the Act.
(2) Where, in the course of investigating an objection case, the chief prosecutor determines that a document prepared by the notary public concerned does not have the effect of notarization pursuant to Article 3 of the Act, the chief prosecutor shall report the case to the Minister of Justice before disposing of it.
